Explanation:
The function `r_bin_java_bootstrap_methods_attr_new` is vulnerable due to the lack of proper bounds checking when reading the bootstrap methods. Specifically, the loop that reads each bootstrap method does not check if the remaining buffer size (`sz - offset`) is sufficient to read the entire bootstrap method. This can lead to buffer over-reads if the input data is crafted maliciously.

The problematic section of the code is:

```c
for (i = 0; i < attr->info.bootstrap_methods_attr.num_bootstrap_methods; i++) {
    if (offset >= sz) {
        break;
    }
    bsm = r_bin_java_bootstrap_method_new(buffer + offset, sz - offset, buf_offset + offset);
    if (bsm) {
        offset += bsm->size;
        r_list_append(attr->info.bootstrap_methods_attr.bootstrap_methods, (void *)bsm);
    } else {
        // TODO eprintf Failed to read the %d boot strap method.
    }
}
```

Here, `r_bin_java_bootstrap_method_new` is called with `buffer + offset` and `sz - offset`, but there is no check to ensure that `bsm->size` does not exceed `sz - offset`. If `bsm->size` is larger than the remaining buffer size, it will result in a buffer over-read.

To fix this vulnerability, the function should include a check to ensure that the size of each bootstrap method does not exceed the remaining buffer size before attempting to read it.

